From 70bdd169848115d54fa780c79458c32c8df08fcd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 15:54:14 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Fit typed table HTML to the scan; lighter annotation overlay

Table HTML typed / pasted by the annotator
- Used to be laid out as an even grid over the table box (every row the
  same height), so cells missed the scan and long text was squeezed.
- Backend: /ocr-table takes `html`; table_structure.fit_html keeps its
  rows / columns / spans / text and places them on the image's ruling
  lines (text rows when a rule is missing), choosing rules that agree
  with the typed spans.
- Editor: after HTML that adds/removes cells, the table is fitted
  automatically (0.9 s after typing stops); "Fit cells to image" button.
  Placeholder lines of the old cells are dropped so none stay behind.
- <br> inside a cell is kept as a line break and written back as <br>
  (editor, export and server HTML).
- Ruling lines are drawn only where a cell has an edge, so they no longer
  cross merged cells.

Overlay
- Layers panel: "Box opacity" slider (default 60 %) and "Show text on
  boxes" (default off - the white text boxes hid the scan). Remembered
  per browser. The selected box is not dimmed.

Tests: 3 new cases with the annotator's form table (13 total).

